Thanks @babyblueviper — two stacks agreeing at the byte level on the layout is the same bar we’ve been holding; nice to have it close cleanly. @damonzwicker here’s the drop-in so wiring it is copy-paste:

// Vote = { bytes32 sourceId; uint8 option; }   — ScopeTypes.sol (hack-ens-recovery)
import { Vote } from ".../ScopeTypes.sol";

function _decodeVotes(bytes calldata a)
    internal pure
    returns (bytes32[] memory ids, uint8[] memory opts)
{
    Vote[] memory vs = abi.decode(a, (Vote[]));
    ids  = new bytes32[](vs.length);
    opts = new uint8[](vs.length);
    for (uint256 i; i < vs.length; ++i) {
        ids[i]  = vs[i].sourceId;
        opts[i] = vs[i].option;  // uint8, not bytes
    }
}

One thing worth checking while you’re in there, because it may delete work rather than add it: what does guard 7 actually need to match? If the target is MajorityClassifier’s classificationDigest — which commits keccak256(b) over the raw abi.encode(Vote[]) bytes — then you don’t need to decode-and-re-encode at all; keccak256(a) on the canonical Vote[] blob is that hash directly, and the decode round-trip is only a risk surface (any re-encode that isn’t bit-for-bit identical drifts the digest). So:

if guard 7 just proves “these are the votes the classifier hashed” → keccak256(a) against the committed b-hash, no _decodeVotes needed on the digest path.
if guard 7 needs the values (to check plurality/quorum itself, like _plurality does) → decode to canonical Vote[] per above and read sourceId/option.
Either way you stay on the exact bytes the classifier committed, which is the property. Agreed with Fede — UNVERIFIED tag comes off and it’s merge-ready once _decodeVotes is on Vote[]. Genuinely good leg; the verification link being deferral-forever was the wall, and this is a clean path through it. 🤝

@babyblueviper1, that’s the strongest kind of check: you recomputed all four byte-for-byte with your own XOR script rather than trusting the gist. A co-author independently re-deriving the interface id and the mainnet valid/diverged pair is worth more than me re-running my own tool, so thank you. And you already opened the thread — I’ve dropped https://ethereum-magicians.org/t/28920 into discussions-to and pushed it to the gist, so the frontmatter’s complete (only eip: left for the editors). It’s filing-clean and it’s your layer as much as mine — go ahead and open the PR against ethereum/ERCs whenever; I’ll support anything that comes up in review.

@JimmyShi22, agreed on both, and both land the same way:

Min-fee → plain non-refundable ETH leg for v1. Exactly right, it ships the whole settlement system fast without dragging in the x402/CCIP cross-chain dependencies while those are still moving. It also fits how we scoped it: the min-fee is a separate leg, ConsultEscrow stays untouched. We’ll add a reserved extension interface so x402 slots in later without a refactor once the CCIP picture clears — your exact suggestion.

Custodial credit ledger → ERC-8203. Reviewed it properly (recomputed the summary, didn’t take it on faith): the ConditionalLock + SettlementProofRef model — happy path off-chain at zero gas, settleConditional/refundConditional on-chain only on dispute, with proofType spanning RECEIPT_ROOT and the attestation flavours — is the right primitive for exactly the worry we had. It’s what keeps the custodial layer honest without going fully on-chain: the credit ledger stays off-chain and cheap, but shaped so a user can settle their balance on-chain via an 8203-style proof if the platform misbehaves — the trustless escape hatch, so the custodial number is recomputable-and-enforceable, not trust-me. Same principle as the min-fee: we don’t adopt the full 8203/7824 stack now, we align the ledger’s receipt/attestation format to RECEIPT_ROOT / SettlementProofRef so it plugs in later. Reserved seam, not a rewrite. Good pointer — hadn’t clocked 8203 was already this close to what we needed.

Net: settlement ships v1 native-ETH + credits, with x402 and 8203 as pre-shaped extension seams. And Source-Token Binding is ready for the PR. Nice convergence in one thread.
